\section*{Patterson first moment over Eisenstein primes}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 023; 2026-09-25), the asserted result is that averaging cubic Gauss sums with the prescribed normalization over the entire primary Eisenstein-prime set gives Patterson's main term, and every fixed nonzero angular mode contributes a negligible amount.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Set $\omega=e^{2\pi i/3}$, $\mathcal O=\mathbb Z[\omega]$, $N(b)=b\bar b$ and $\operatorname{Tr}(z)=z+\bar z$. For a primary element $b\equiv1\pmod3$, let $\chi_b$ be its cubic residue symbol, zero on nonunits, and define
\[
 G(b)=N(b)^{-1/2}\sum_{v\bmod b}\chi_b(v)e^{2\pi i\operatorname{Tr}(v/b)},\qquad
 c_*=(2\pi)^{2/3}/(3\Gamma(2/3)).
\]
The claimed unconditional sharp-cutoff first moment is
\[
 \sum_{\substack{N\pi\le X\\\pi\equiv1\pmod3\text{ prime}}}G(\pi)
 =\tfrac65c_*X^{5/6}/\log X+o(X^{5/6}/\log X)\quad(X\to\infty).
\]
All primary prime elements are included, in particular both conjugate primes above a split rational prime.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 023; 2026-09-25), angular Hecke characters and a Voronoi transformation give Type I estimates. Corrected dispersion and exceptional-moment bounds are joined to an exact prime decomposition, and smoothing is removed only after each fixed angular mode has been controlled.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 023; 2026-09-25).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 023; 2026-09-25), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: averaging cubic Gauss sums with the prescribed normalization over the entire primary Eisenstein-prime set gives Patterson's main term, and every fixed nonzero angular mode contributes a negligible amount.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PattersonFirstMoment.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.CubicFirstMoment.patterson\_angularCancellation}, \texttt{OAI.CubicFirstMoment.patterson\_angularComparison}, \texttt{OAI.CubicFirstMoment.patterson\_firstMoment}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

